      "body": "## Motivation\n\nOn HIP, `sparse_mla_fwd_decode_partial` is used for both prefill and decode. For invalid (`-1`) top-k indices it loaded KV row 0 and relied on a `-inf` score mask. Padded CUDA-graph rows write their K/V into row 0 (`out_cache_loc` padding is 0). Once row 0 held a non-finite value, `-inf + q·k` and `0 * v` became NaN for every later request.\n\nWith BF16 KV and decode CUDA graphs, the server was correct until the first decode batch whose size had no captured graph (for example 3 requests replayed in the batch-size-4 graph). After that, every request returned the right first token followed by `!!!!...` and `None` logprobs until the server restarted. Eager decode and FP8 KV were not affected. `SGLANG_ENABLE_WAR_BARRIER=1` did not help.\n\nThis showed up on GLM-5.3-Flash BF16 RL rollout on MI355X and is independent of the model: any BF16 sparse-MLA decode that uses this kernel and CUDA graphs can poison the server.\n\n## Modifications\n\nIn `sparse_mla_fwd_decode_partial`, masked slots now load zeros instead of row 0, in both the main and tail parts. The score mask is unchanged. The FP8 kernel (`sparse_mla_fwd_decode_partial_fp8`) has the same load pattern and is not changed here.\n\n## Accuracy Tests\n\nOn a fresh MI355X server with BF16 KV and decode CUDA graphs, padded batches of 3 and 5 were correct, and so was every request afterward. After 128 concurrent requests with 4K outputs, single-request and 32-request probes were still correct. Teacher-forced logprobs against the previous torch sparse-MLA path differed by about 0.05 per token with no directional bias.\n\n## Speed Tests and Profiling\n\nMI355X, one TP8 engine, BF16, TileLang DSA, AITER MoE, decode CUDA graphs up to batch size 128: 3,575 tok/s (17.4 ms/step) at 64 requests of 512 in / 1024 out, and 4,215 tok/s (21.3 ms/step) at 128 requests of 512 in / 4096 out. The zero-fill replaces a load of row 0; it is not a throughput change.\n\n## Checklist\n\n- [x] Format your code according to the [Format code with pre-commit](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#format-code-with-pre-commit).\n- [ ] Add unit tests according to the [Run and add unit tests](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#run-and-add-unit-tests).\n- [ ] Update documentation according to [Write documentations](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#write-documentations).\n- [x] Provide accuracy and speed benchmark results according to [Test the accuracy](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#test-the-accuracy) and [Benchmark the speed](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#benchmark-the-speed).\n- [x] Follow the SGLang code style [guidance](https://docs.sglang.io/developer_guide/contribution_guide.html#code-style-guidance).\n\n\nMade with [Cursor](https://cursor.com)\n\n<!-- pr-states:start -->\n---\n### CI States\n\nLatest PR Test (Base): <!-- slot:pr-test:start -->:x: [Run #38785845775](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/38785845775)<!-- slot:pr-test:end -->\nLatest PR Test (Extra): <!-- slot:pr-test-extra:start -->:x: [Run #38785845797](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/38785845797)<!-- slot:pr-test-extra:end -->\nLatest PR Test (AMD ROCm 10): <!-- slot:pr-test-amd:start -->:x: [Run #38785845577](https://github.com/sgl-project/sglang/actions/runs/38785845577)<!-- slot:pr-test-amd:end -->\n<!-- pr-states:end -->",
